    summary: "Frustrated by a people who would not listen, Yunus عليه السلام left them before Allah told him to — and found himself thrown overboard in a storm, swallowed by a whale, and calling out from three layers of darkness at once, even as the very people he had abandoned became the only nation in the Quran recorded to have believed in time to save themselves.",
    story: `Prophet Yunus عليه السلام, also known as Dhan-Nun, was sent to the town of Nineveh, whose people were idolaters living shameless lives. Allah the Almighty said of them: "Was there any town that believed after seeing the punishment, and whose faith saved it? None — except the people of Yunus. When they believed, We removed from them the torment of disgrace in the life of this world, and let them enjoy life for a while." (Quran 10:98)

Yunus عليه السلام preached to them the worship of Allah, but they resented his interference, insisting: "We and our forefathers have worshipped these gods for generations, and no harm has ever come to us." However he tried to show them the foolishness of their idolatry and the goodness of Allah's law, they ignored him, and when he warned that Allah's punishment would follow if they persisted, they told him plainly they were not afraid of his threats: "Let it happen," they said. Disheartened, Yunus عليه السلام told them, "Then I will leave you to your misery," and left Nineveh, fearing Allah's anger was about to fall on them — but leaving before he had been given permission to do so. Allah the Almighty said of this: "And remember Dhan-Nun, when he went off in anger, and thought that We would not put him in difficulty." (Quran 21:87)

He had barely left the city when the sky began to change color, as though it were on fire, and the people, terrified, remembered what had become of the peoples of 'Ad, Thamud, and Nuh before them. Faith slowly took hold of their hearts; they gathered on the mountain and cried out to Allah for mercy and forgiveness, in a moment of sincere, collective repentance — and Allah lifted His wrath from them, restoring His blessings, after which they prayed for Yunus's return to guide them once more.

Yunus عليه السلام, meanwhile, had boarded a small ship with other passengers. It sailed calmly through the day, but as night fell, a violent storm broke — waves rising like mountains and plunging like valleys, sweeping the deck and threatening to tear the ship apart. Behind it, unseen, a great whale had been commanded by Allah to rise and follow the ship.

As the storm worsened, the crew began throwing cargo overboard to lighten the load, but it was not enough — they resolved among themselves that a person would have to go as well, and the captain proposed drawing lots among the travelers, the one chosen to be cast into the sea. This was an old seafaring custom, rooted in the polytheistic belief that the sea and wind answered to gods that needed appeasing. Reluctantly, Yunus عليه السلام agreed to take part. The lot was drawn, and his name came up. Knowing him to be the most honorable man aboard, the others could not bring themselves to throw him overboard, and drew again — his name came up a second time, and then, on a third and final draw, a third time.

Yunus عليه السلام understood at once that Allah's hand was behind it — he had left his mission without leave to do so. Accepting what had to happen, he stood at the ship's edge, facing the raging, moonless sea beneath a sky hidden by black fog, and, calling on Allah's name, threw himself into the water and vanished beneath the waves.

The whale found him floating and swallowed him whole, its jaws closing over him like bolts locking a prison door, then dove to the depths of the sea. Three layers of darkness closed over Yunus عليه السلام at once — the darkness of the whale's belly, the darkness of the ocean floor, and the darkness of night itself. For a moment he believed himself dead, until he realized he could still move, and understood he was alive, imprisoned within three darknesses together. His heart turned at once to Allah, and his tongue was loosed to say: "There is no god but You — glory be to You, I have indeed been among the wrongdoers." (Quran 21:87)

He repeated this call again and again. The fish, whales, seaweed, and every creature of the sea heard his voice glorifying Allah from within the whale's own stomach, and gathered around it, joining in the praise of Allah each in its own way. Even the whale itself joined in, understanding it had swallowed a Prophet, and, though frightened at first, reassured itself: "Why should I fear? Allah commanded me to swallow him."

Allah, witnessing Yunus's sincere repentance and hearing his call from within the whale, commanded the whale to rise and cast him onto an island, far across the sea, toward warmth and open land. Yunus عليه السلام emerged with his body inflamed from the whale's digestive acids, weak and ill, the sun's rays burning against his raw skin until the pain was almost unbearable — yet he endured it, repeating his call to Allah throughout. Allah caused a vine to grow up around him for shade and protection, restored his health, and forgave him, telling him that had he not called upon Allah as he did, he would have remained in the whale's belly until the Day of Resurrection.

Allah the Almighty recounted: "And verily, Yunus was one of the Messengers. When he fled to the laden ship, he agreed to cast lots, and he was among the losers. Then a fish swallowed him, and he had done an act worthy of blame. Had he not been of those who glorify Allah, he would have remained inside its belly until the Day of Resurrection. But We cast him onto a bare shore while he was sick, and We caused a gourd plant to grow over him. And We sent him to a hundred thousand people or more, and they believed — so We gave them enjoyment for a while." (Quran 37:139-148)

Regaining his strength, Yunus عليه السلام made his way back to Nineveh, and was met with an astonishing change: the entire population came out to welcome him, telling him they had come to believe in Allah in his absence. Together, they offered a prayer of thanksgiving to their Merciful Lord.

Ibn 'Abbas narrated that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: "One should not say that I am better than Yunus ibn Matta."`,
