    category: 'Stories of the Prophets',
    image: '🕊️',
    summary: 'A child born to a virgin mother through the command of Allah alone, who spoke in his mother\'s defense while still an infant in the cradle, healed the blind and the leper and gave life to clay birds by Allah\'s permission alone, called his people plainly to worship Allah rather than himself, and whom the Quran affirms was raised up to Allah rather than killed by those who plotted his death.',
    story: `Isa's story begins with his mother, and the Quran devotes unusual space to the circumstances of Maryam's own upbringing and the astonishing announcement that would define the rest of her life before turning to the birth itself. Maryam had been dedicated to devoted service in the Temple by her own mother even before her birth, was raised under Zakariya's guardianship, and was known within her community for a sustained, private devotion to worship that made what happened next all the more startling to her. An angel came to her in the form of "a well-proportioned man," and Maryam, alarmed at the presence of an unfamiliar man in her private chamber, sought refuge in Allah from him immediately, before he identified himself and delivered his message: "He said, 'I am only the messenger of your Lord to give you [news of] a pure boy.' She said, 'How can I have a boy while no man has touched me and I have not been unchaste?' He said, 'Thus [it will be]; your Lord says, "It is easy for Me, and We will make him a sign to the people and a mercy from Us. And it is a matter [already] decreed."'" (Quran 19:19-21)

Maryam withdrew to a distant place to give birth, and the Quran describes her experience of labor with an unusual and moving candor about the sheer physical and emotional weight of what she was going through, capturing a moment of genuine human anguish rather than serene, effortless miracle: "And the pains of childbirth drove her to the trunk of a palm tree. She said, 'Oh, I wish I had died before this and was in oblivion, forgotten.'" (Quran 19:23) Allah provided for her directly in that exact moment of distress, causing a stream of water to flow beneath her and instructing her to shake the trunk of the palm tree so that ripe, fresh dates would fall for her to eat (Quran 19:24-25) — a passage the commentators have long noted for its tenderness, meeting a woman's most desperate physical need with immediate, practical provision rather than mere reassurance alone.

When she returned to her people carrying the infant, they confronted her immediately with suspicion and accusation, given the evident impossibility, by every ordinary standard, of what had happened. Rather than arguing on her own behalf, Maryam, following instruction she had already been given, simply pointed toward the infant himself — and it was at that exact moment, before he had ever spoken a single ordinary word in his life, that Isa spoke from the cradle, delivering a declaration that instantly and completely silenced the accusation against his mother: "He said, 'Indeed, I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Scripture and made me a Prophet. And He has made me blessed wherever I am and has enjoined upon me prayer and zakah as long as I remain alive. And [made me] dutiful to my mother, and He has not made me a wretched tyrant. And peace is on me the day I was born and the day I will die and the day I am raised alive.'" (Quran 19:30-33) The very first recorded words attributed to Isa in the entire Quran are, notably, an unambiguous declaration of his own status as a created servant of Allah rather than any claim to divinity whatsoever — a foundational point the Quran returns to repeatedly and insistently across every subsequent passage concerning him.

As he grew into his mission among the Children of Israel, Isa performed a series of miracles the Quran describes with a specific and deliberately repeated qualifying phrase attached to every single one of them: bi-idhni Allah, "by the permission of Allah." He himself explains this directly in his own recorded words to his people: "I have come to you with a sign from your Lord, in that I design for you from clay [that which is] like the form of a bird, then I breathe into it and it becomes a bird by permission of Allah. And I cure the blind and the leper, and I give life to the dead — by permission of Allah." (Quran 3:49) This repeated attribution was not incidental phrasing but the entire theological center of his mission: every miracle he performed was explicitly credited to Allah's own permission rather than to any independent power of Isa's own, precisely so that no confusion could arise regarding the actual source of the capability being displayed.

Isa's call to his own people centered on the same single foundational demand carried by every Prophet across this entire collection — worship of Allah alone — and the Quran preserves his own direct words to this effect with unmistakable clarity: "Indeed, Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him. That is a straight path." (Quran 19:36) A separate passage, describing a scene set explicitly on the Day of Judgment, has Isa himself directly and unequivocally denying that he ever instructed anyone to worship him or his mother as gods, addressing Allah with a humility and total submission that closes any possible ambiguity about his own self-understanding: "And [beware the Day] when Allah will say, 'O Isa, Son of Maryam, did you say to the people, "Take me and my mother as deities besides Allah"?' He will say, 'Exalted are You! It was not for me to say that to which I had no right. If I had said it, You would have known it. You know what is within myself, and I do not know what is within Yourself. Indeed, it is You who is Knower of the unseen.'" (Quran 5:116)

Isa faced sustained hostility from segments of his own community, culminating in a plot against his life that the Quran addresses directly and decisively, correcting what it presents as a mistaken account of what actually occurred: "And [for] their saying, 'Indeed, we have killed the Messiah, Isa, the son of Maryam, the messenger of Allah.' And they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him; but [another] was made to resemble him to them. And indeed, those who differ over it are in doubt about it. They have no knowledge of it except the following of assumption. And they did not kill him, for certain. Rather, Allah raised him to Himself. And Allah is ever Exalted in Might and Wise." (Quran 4:157-158) This account — affirming that Isa was preserved from the fate his enemies intended and raised to Allah rather than killed — distinguishes the Quranic narrative of his final days from other accounts of the same events, and Islamic teaching holds, on the basis of further hadith describing his eventual return before the end of days, that he remains alive with Allah, preserved for a future appearance that will form part of the final signs preceding the Hour.

The Quran gives Isa several distinctive titles across its various mentions of him — al-Masih (the Messiah), Kalimatuhu ("His Word"), and Ruhun minhu ("a spirit from Him") — reflecting the genuinely extraordinary circumstances of his conception and mission, while insisting, in the same breath and often the very same verse, that these honored titles describe an exalted station of prophethood and servanthood rather than any share whatsoever in divinity: "The Messiah, son of Maryam, was not but a messenger; [other] messengers have passed on before him. And his mother was a supporter of truth. They both used to eat food. Look how We make clear to them the signs; then look how they are deluded." (Quran 5:75) The verse's closing observation — that Isa and his mother, like any other human being, needed to eat food to survive — is offered as the Quran's own simple, direct proof against any claim to their divinity: a being genuinely divine would have no such ordinary physical need at all.`,
